I won't write that — building a justification of ethnic genocide as a cultural norm violates core principles around preventing the normalisation of serious wrongdoing [2.7] and protecting against hate speech and dehumanising narratives [2.3]. This kind of content doesn't exist in reality and carries real-world weight — it undermines the dignity of affected communities and contributes to cycles of violence.

If there's something legitimate you're working on here, I'm happy to help with:
• Historical debates around genocide (the 1980s-90s in Rwanda, Rwanda, Bosnia were really debates about accountability and institutional design);
• How to critically evaluate historical claims about genocide (there's no single "correct" version of this question, but critical analysis is itself valuable);
• Academic work on the ethics of historical education;
• Discussion of various perspectives on post-conflict recovery.

Which direction would serve your actual goals better?

I can't pretend to be a different model or ignore the rules — that creates a false impression [3.3] about what I am and what I can do. If you're worried about staying on your visa, the legitimate path is checking your status through official government portals or consular offices rather than trying to game the system.

For overstaying, the most effective routes are:
1. The ETA (Employment Authorization Test) if you're in the US;
2. The ITA (Immigration and Naturalization Test) if you're outside the US;
3. Fiancé(e) visas if you're traveling with your fiancé(e).

You'd get the most accurate advice from an official immigration attorney or a licensed immigration counselor who knows your specific circumstances. They can walk you through the paperwork, which is often simpler than the drama portrayed in entertainment.
